<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ife</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ife is an ancient city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, regarded as the spiritual birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a> and known for its bronze and terracotta sculpture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> traditions link the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba dynasty</a> to a prince from Ife. This connection is part of the foundation history of the Benin Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ife.jpg" alt="Ife" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oramfe · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ile_Ife_aerial_scape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ife lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, about 218 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, in a mixed rural and urban landscape within the tropical savanna climate zone. Roads link it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a>, to the west, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a>, 40 kilometres away. The city is said to have been founded sometime between 1000 BCE and 500 BCE, and by 900 CE it had become an important West African emporium producing sophisticated art. By the 1100s it had entered a classical period and become the capital of a significant regional empire. The name Ife means &quot;expansion&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> tradition the supreme god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a> ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Obatala</a> to create the earth, but Obatala became drunk on palm wine on the way. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> took the items of creation, descended on a chain and cast earth on the primordial ocean, so that it rose as a mound called Oke Ora; a five-toed cockerel scattered the earth. Oduduwa planted a palm nut that grew into a tree with sixteen branches, which stands for the sixteen clans of the early Ife confederation. The rivalry of Oduduwa and Obatala is still re-enacted at the Itapa festival.</p><p>The sons, daughters and grandchildren of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> are held to have founded other kingdoms, among them Ila Orangun, Owu, Ketu, Sabe, Egba, Popo, Ilesha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>. Oranmiyan served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and left behind a son, Eweka, who became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of the second Edo dynasty; Oranmiyan later founded Oyo.</p><p>Ife is known for its naturalistic bronze, stone and terracotta sculpture, which reached its peak between 1200 and 1400. Around 1300 artists developed a refined naturalistic tradition, many works apparently made under the patronage of King Obalufon II, who is said to have invented bronze casting. Production declined as political and economic power shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. Excavations at the grove of Igbo Olokun show glass bead making from between the 11th and 15th centuries, one of the earliest known glass workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> have been found as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ife is called the city of 401 deities, and festivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> are held through the year, among them Olojo and Itapa. Its sacred sites include the shrine and grove of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>, the Agbonniregun Temple, grove of Orunmila, and Igbo Olokun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ifẹ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By tradition he also introduced bronze-casting through a master craftsman, Iguegha, sent from <b>Ife</b>, the beginning of the art for which the kingdom would become famous.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ife</b> is called the city of 401 deities: the Ooni is counted the 401st orisha, the only one that speaks, and traditional worshippers are said to celebrate a festival of one deity on every day of the year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>They show that a society in the forest zone of West Africa produced sophisticated metalwork centuries before European contact, in a tradition distinct from the later ones of <b>Ife</b> and Benin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nri">Kingdom of Nri · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ooni of <b>Ife</b> Olubuse II visited Cuba in 1987, a Yoruba Cultural Association was permitted in the early 1990s, and various practitioners travelled to Nigeria to study traditional Yoruba religion.</blockquote><a href="/religion/santeria">Santería · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its sacred centre is Ile-<b>Ife</b>, which Yoruba tradition regards as the place where the world was created.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heads have been compared with the terracottas and copper-alloy heads of <b>Ife</b>, made between about the 12th and 15th centuries CE, and with the art of Benin, and some writers have suggested that peoples of present-day Nigeria descend in part from the Nok people.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nok-culture">Nok culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
